{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210078,"nonce":"O3/A7MSserkoybJH8HHxVgexN80=","title":"infer_return_type_from_body takes the FIRST return site, even when a later one is the informative one","message":"function parseBlock(tokens:List\u003cToken\u003e) {\n        var l = new List();\n        ...\n        if (l.length == 1) return l.first();   // Null\u003cT\u003e, T still the formal\n        return OpBlock(l);                     // TemplateExpr\n    }\n\n`infer_return_type_from_body` (compiler/src/tast/ast_lowering/infer.rs)\nreturns the first return type it finds. Here that is `l.first()`'s `Null\u003cT\u003e`\nwith T the List's own formal parameter, which converts to a BOX (`*u8`). The\nfunction is typed as returning a box; the constructor stores that box into\n`expr:TemplateExpr` and switches on it. The Int box's tag is 3, and OpStr is\nconstructor index 3, so the switch even \"matches\" -- with an empty payload.\nThis is why every haxe.Template.execute returned \"\".\n\nA measured fix -- prefer the first return site whose type is informative (not a\nTypeParameter, bare or under Null, not Dynamic/Unknown) -- makes parseBlock\nreturn the enum (`-\u003e i64`), but then \"plain text\" templates CRASH at\nfault=0x100 instead of returning \"\": the next link, not yet diagnosed. HELD\nBACK for that reason. The diff is small (a `return_type_is_informative`\npredicate and a loop that prefers it).\n\nRelated: `Null\u003cE\u003e` for an enum E with a payload variant is boxed by\nconvert_type (mir/types.rs) because E's IrType is I64, the same test as Int.\nBut such enum values are ALWAYS heap objects (even a payload-less variant of a\nmixed enum allocates; only a pure discriminant enum is a bare int), so the box\nis unnecessary and inconsistent. `enum_is_boxed(symbol)` is the predicate to\ngate on. Also held back, untested alone.","files":null}]}